{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. His early years as count were marked by regency and political instability as powerful nobles vied for control over the young ruler. The kingdom of Jerusalem which was closely tied to Tripoli through marriage and military alliances played a major role in shaping his early reign. Raymond was raised in the court of his uncle King Baldwin III and later under the guidance of his mother who acted as regent until he came of age.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became known for his cautious and often controversial diplomatic approach. He was deeply involved in the politics of the Crusader states and frequently clashed with other nobles including the King of Jerusalem. He was accused of being too friendly with Muslim rulers and even suspected of treason during the reign of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was one of the few nobles who opposed the aggressive policies of Guy of Lusignan and the military orders. His refusal to support the disastrous campaign at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 was seen by some as a betrayal but by others as a prudent decision to preserve his forces. He was also a patron of the arts and helped strengthen the infrastructure of Tripoli during his long rule.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin. His death came at a time of great crisis for the Crusader states. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV who was still a minor and thus the county fell under the control of regents. Raymond III left behind a complex legacy. He was neither a great warrior nor a charismatic leader but 